## Appended by Stage 2a-3, 2026-09-13: the record metadata sidecar

`data/raw/ec3_record_metadata_2026-08-14.csv.gz` carries the per-record metadata
that the frozen ECC extract does not: the EC3 category path, the declared unit
and its type for EVERY usable record including the 2,780 outside each category's
majority unit type, and the product name. It exists so the category-split
decision in `src/categorysplit.py` can be audited without the store, and so the
finding that EC3 carries no subcategory for these records is verifiable.

It is NOT an analysis input. `src/empirical.py` reads only the frozen ECC
extract, which already carries every field the split uses. Built and verified by
`audits/stage2a3/q1_rebuild_slice.py`, which reconstructs the 2026-08 store
slice and refuses to write unless the majority-unit subset reproduces the frozen
extract record for record: 120,280 of 120,280 identical ids and categories, ECC
agreeing to a worst relative difference of 8.9e-13 (the frozen file is CSV text,
so the comparison is at round-trip precision, not bitwise).

Frozen because the store is append-only and has moved on: the slice is
recoverable only while the 2026-08-13/14 rows remain in it.

CORRECTION to `ec3_raw_ecc_2026-08-14_runmeta.json`, same stage. It carried
`api_pull_result: HTTP 403`, asserting that direct EC3 API access was closed to
this account. That was wrong: the key in use at the time was stale and a working
key has since been set. The false fields are removed and `source_kind` now says
what is true. No byte of the extract changed, so its own checksum is unchanged;
the runmeta's checksum below supersedes the one above.

